''truppa'' (ML. ''troppus, tropus''), a company, troop; origin unknown. According to Diez, a change, in the mouth of Germans, from L. ''turba'' into *''trupa'', whence, by change of gender, ''tropus, troppus''. Cf. ''tropel''.] 1. An assemblage of people; a multitude; a company; a band.

{{smaller block|We come by ''troops'' to the place of assembly, that, being banded as it were together, we may be supplicants enough to besiege God with our prayers.
{{right|''Tertullian'', quoted in Hooker's Eccles. Polity, v. 24.}}}}

{{smaller block|Honour, love, obedience, ''troops'' of friends, I must not look to have.
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, v. 3. 25.}}}}

{{smaller block|There was a ''troup'' o' gentlemen
Came riding merrilie by.
{{right|''The Broom of Cowdenknows'' (Child's Ballads, IV. 45).}}}}

2. A body of soldiers: generally used in the plural, signifying soldiers in general, whether more or less numerous, and whether belonging to the infantry, cavalry, or artillery.

{{smaller block|Farewell the plumed ''troop'', and the big wars,
That make ambition virtue!
{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, iii. 3. 349.}}}}

{{smaller block|Colonel Prendergast, the commandant of the station, had but 800 ''troops'', of whom 200 only were Europeans, to meet a force of overwhelming superiority in numbers.
{{right|''Cornhill Mag.'', Oct., 1888, p. 380.}}}}

3. In ''cavalry'', the unit of formation, consisting usually of sixty troopers, commanded by a captain, and corresponding to a company of infantry.

{{smaller block|When a ''troop'' dismounts and acts on foot, it is still called by that name.
{{right|''Stocqueler.''}}}}

