{{smaller block|But, whatever she had to say for herself, she was at last forced to ''troop'' off.{{right|''Addison'', Spectator, No. 464.}}}}

{{smaller block|He was generally seen ''trooping'' like a colt at his mother's heels.{{right|''Irving'', Sketch-Book, p. 48.}}}}

4†. To associate or consort.

{{smaller block|A snowy dove ''trooping'' with crows.{{right|''Shak.'', R. and J., i. 5. 50.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To associate as in a troop or company.

{{smaller block|To ''troope'' my selfe with such a crew of men<br>As shall so fill the downes of Affrica.{{right|''Greene'', Orlando Furioso, l. 213.}}}}

2. To form into troops, as a regiment.—'''Trooping the colors''', {{smaller|in the British army, an elaborate ceremony performed at the public mounting of garrison guards.}}

