'''trooper''' (trö′pėr), ''n.'' [= F. ''troupier''; as ''troop'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. A private soldier in a body of cavalry; a horse-soldier.

{{smaller block|The ''troopers'', according to custom, fired without having dismounted.{{right|''Scott'', Old Mortality, xvi.}}}}

2. A cavalry horse; a troop-horse.—'''3.''' A troop-ship.—'''Native trooper''', {{smaller|in Australia, a member of a body of mounted police recruited from the aborigines and officered by white men.}}—'''Trooper's damn.''' {{smaller|See ''damn''.}}

